{"questions": [{"id": "ma-1.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Scarcity exists because", "choices": ["prices are too high", "governments restrict production", "some goods are free", "money is limited", "human wants exceed the resources available to satisfy them"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Scarcity is the gap between unlimited wants and limited resources. It exists in every economy regardless of prices or money.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-1.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A point inside a country's production possibilities curve indicates that the country", "choices": ["is producing efficiently", "cannot reach that point", "has experienced economic growth", "has some unemployed or underused resources", "is specializing completely"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Points inside the curve are attainable but inefficient: the economy could produce more of at least one good without giving up the other.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-1.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following would shift a country's production possibilities curve outward?", "choices": ["An improvement in production technology", "A decrease in unemployment", "A movement from inside the curve to the curve", "A shift of resources from one good to another", "An increase in consumer demand"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Better technology raises what the economy can produce with the same resources. Lower unemployment only moves the economy toward the existing curve.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-1.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A production possibilities curve that is bowed outward from the origin shows that", "choices": ["opportunity cost is constant", "resources are equally suited to producing both goods", "the economy is inefficient", "opportunity cost rises as more of a good is produced", "no trade-offs exist"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Resources are specialized, so moving more of them into one good pulls in resources that are progressively worse at producing it.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-1.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The table shows the maximum amount of corn or steel each country can produce with the same resources. Which of the following is true?", "choices": ["Alpha has a comparative advantage in corn, and Beta has a comparative advantage in steel.", "Alpha has a comparative advantage in steel.", "Beta has a comparative advantage in corn.", "Beta has an absolute advantage in both goods.", "Neither country gains from trade."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Alpha gives up 60/20 = 3 tons of corn per ton of steel; Beta gives up 30/30 = 1. Beta is the low-cost steel producer, so Alpha specializes in corn.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20330%20108%27%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ECorn%20%28tons%29%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESteel%20%28tons%29%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EAlpha%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E60%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E20%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EBeta%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E30%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E30%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2796%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2796%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-1.1-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "Using the same table, which terms of trade for one ton of steel would benefit both countries?", "choices": ["0.5 ton of corn", "1 ton of corn", "2 tons of corn", "3 tons of corn", "4 tons of corn"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Beta will sell steel for more than 1 ton of corn and Alpha will pay less than 3. Two tons of corn falls strictly between those limits.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20330%20108%27%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ECorn%20%28tons%29%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESteel%20%28tons%29%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EAlpha%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E60%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E20%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EBeta%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E30%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E30%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2796%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2796%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-1.1-07", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Compared with a country that devotes most of its resources to consumer goods, a country that devotes more resources to capital goods today will most likely", "choices": ["have a higher standard of living today", "face lower opportunity costs", "have more unemployment in the future", "move inside its production possibilities curve", "experience faster growth in its production possibilities in the future"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Capital goods raise future productive capacity, so the curve shifts out faster over time, at the cost of less consumption now.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-1.1-08", "subject": "macro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "An economy moves along its production possibilities curve from producing 10 tractors and 50 tons of food to 20 tractors and 35 tons of food. What is the opportunity cost of each additional tractor?", "choices": ["0.67 ton of food", "1.5 tons of food", "3.5 tons of food", "15 tons of food", "35 tons of food"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Ten more tractors cost 50 − 35 = 15 tons of food, so each tractor costs 15/10 = 1.5 tons.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The table shows data for a country in billions of dollars. What is the value of the country's imports?", "choices": ["$3 billion", "$5 billion", "$7 billion", "$10 billion", "$17 billion"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "GDP = C + I + G + X − M, so 70 = 40 + 12 + 15 + 10 − M, which gives M = $7 billion.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20407%20192%27%20width%3D%27407%27%20height%3D%27192%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27407%27%20height%3D%27192%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27397%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EComponent%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EBillions%20of%20dollars%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27397%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EConsumer%20spending%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2440%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EInvestment%20spending%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2412%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EGovernment%20spending%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2415%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EExports%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2410%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27113.0%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EGross%20domestic%20product%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27306.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2470%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%27180%27%20x2%3D%27397%27%20y2%3D%27180%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-2.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is included in this year's GDP?", "choices": ["The sale of a used car", "A government Social Security payment", "The purchase of shares of stock", "Flour bought by a bakery to make bread", "A new house built and sold this year"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "GDP counts final goods and services newly produced this year. Used goods, transfers, financial assets, and intermediate goods are excluded.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Intermediate goods are excluded from GDP in order to", "choices": ["avoid counting the same production twice", "measure only government output", "adjust for inflation", "exclude imports", "measure income inequality"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The value of intermediate goods is already included in the price of the final good, so counting them separately would double count.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following is a limitation of using real GDP per capita to measure a country's well-being?", "choices": ["It includes the value of imports", "It counts transfer payments", "It is adjusted for inflation", "It includes services", "It does not account for leisure or environmental damage"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "GDP measures market output, so it misses the value of free time and the costs of pollution, both of which affect well-being.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The table shows nominal GDP and the GDP deflator for two years. By what percentage did real GDP grow from Year 1 to Year 2?", "choices": ["10%", "12%", "20%", "32%", "0%"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Real GDP is nominal GDP divided by the deflator times 100: $500 in Year 1 and $660/1.2 = $550 in Year 2. Growth is 50/500 = 10%.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20330%20108%27%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27330%27%20height%3D%27108%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EYear%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ENominal%20GDP%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EGDP%20deflator%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E1%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%24500%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E100%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2745.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E2%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%24660%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27258.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E120%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2796%27%20x2%3D%27320%27%20y2%3D%2796%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-2.2-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A country has 90 million employed people, 10 million unemployed people, and 50 million adults not in the labor force. What is the unemployment rate?", "choices": ["6.25%", "10%", "11.1%", "33.3%", "40%"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The labor force is 90 + 10 = 100 million. The unemployment rate is 10/100 = 10%. People not in the labor force are not counted.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.2-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A recent college graduate searching for her first job is an example of", "choices": ["structural unemployment", "cyclical unemployment", "seasonal unemployment", "frictional unemployment", "someone not in the labor force"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Frictional unemployment is the short-term unemployment of people moving between jobs or entering the labor force.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.2-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A worker loses her job because a new machine performs her task and her skills no longer match available jobs. She is", "choices": ["frictionally unemployed", "cyclically unemployed", "seasonally unemployed", "structurally unemployed", "underemployed but counted as employed"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Structural unemployment comes from a mismatch between workers' skills and the skills employers need.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.2-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Workers who are laid off because total spending in the economy falls during a recession are", "choices": ["frictionally unemployed", "structurally unemployed", "discouraged workers", "part of the natural rate of unemployment", "cyclically unemployed"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Cyclical unemployment rises when the economy contracts and falls when it expands. It is not part of the natural rate.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.2-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "When discouraged workers stop looking for work, the official unemployment rate", "choices": ["understates true joblessness", "overstates true unemployment", "is unaffected", "rises", "measures only cyclical unemployment"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Discouraged workers leave the labor force and are not counted as unemployed, so the official rate falls even though they still want jobs.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.3-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The table shows prices and quantities for a two-good market basket. Using Year 1 as the base year, what is the consumer price index in Year 2?", "choices": ["100", "110", "115", "120", "125"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The basket costs 5($6) + 10($2) = $50 in Year 1 and 5($7) + 10($2.50) = $60 in Year 2. The CPI is 60/50 × 100 = 120.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20509%20108%27%20width%3D%27509%27%20height%3D%27108%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27509%27%20height%3D%27108%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27499%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2747.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27143.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EPizza%20price%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27250.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EPizza%20qty%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27354.0%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESoda%20price%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27453.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESoda%20qty%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27499%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2747.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EYear%201%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27143.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%246.00%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27250.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E5%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27354.0%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%242.00%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27453.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E10%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2747.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EYear%202%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27143.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%247.00%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27250.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E5%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27354.0%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%242.50%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27453.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E10%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2796%27%20x2%3D%27499%27%20y2%3D%2796%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-2.3-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "If the consumer price index rises from 150 to 165, the inflation rate is", "choices": ["5%", "10%", "15%", "65%", "165%"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Inflation is (165 − 150)/150 = 15/150 = 10%.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.3-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "If the nominal interest rate is 7 percent and the inflation rate is 4 percent, the real interest rate is approximately", "choices": ["−3%", "3%", "4%", "11%", "28%"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The real interest rate is the nominal rate minus inflation: 7% − 4% = 3%.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.3-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Unexpectedly high inflation will most likely benefit", "choices": ["lenders who made fixed-rate loans", "savers holding cash", "people on fixed incomes", "banks that charge fixed interest", "borrowers who took out fixed-rate loans"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Borrowers repay with money worth less than they expected, so the real value of their debt falls at lenders' expense.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.3-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The consumer price index tends to overstate the true rise in the cost of living because", "choices": ["it includes only services", "it excludes food and energy", "it uses current-year quantities", "consumers substitute toward goods whose prices have risen less", "it adjusts for quality improvements too much"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The CPI holds the basket fixed, but consumers switch away from goods that became relatively expensive. This substitution bias makes measured inflation too high.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.4-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.4", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "During the expansion phase of the business cycle, real GDP and unemployment typically change in which ways?", "choices": ["Real GDP increases and unemployment increases", "Real GDP decreases and unemployment increases", "Real GDP increases and unemployment decreases", "Real GDP decreases and unemployment decreases", "Both stay the same"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "In an expansion, output grows and firms hire more workers, so unemployment falls.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.4-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.4", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A recession is best described as a period in which", "choices": ["real GDP declines", "the price level rises rapidly", "unemployment falls below its natural rate", "the money supply grows", "exports exceed imports"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A recession is a significant decline in real economic activity, usually measured by falling real GDP.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.4-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.4", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The lowest point of the business cycle, after which the economy begins to recover, is called the", "choices": ["trough", "peak", "expansion", "recession", "boom"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The trough marks the end of a contraction and the start of the next expansion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-2.4-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.4", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "At the peak of the business cycle, the economy is most likely to have", "choices": ["unemployment above its natural rate", "output below its full-employment level", "falling prices", "rising cyclical unemployment", "output above its full-employment level and upward pressure on prices"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "At the peak, spending is strong enough to push output past potential, which tightens labor markets and raises inflationary pressure.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following helps explain why the aggregate demand curve slopes downward?", "choices": ["A higher price level raises the real value of wealth, increasing spending", "A higher price level lowers interest rates", "A higher price level makes exports cheaper", "A higher price level lowers the real value of wealth, decreasing consumption", "A higher price level increases government spending"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The real wealth effect: when prices rise, money and other assets buy less, so households cut consumption.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "An increase in consumer confidence will most likely", "choices": ["shift aggregate demand to the left", "shift short-run aggregate supply to the right", "shift aggregate demand to the right", "shift long-run aggregate supply to the left", "cause a movement along aggregate demand"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "More confident households spend more at every price level, so aggregate demand increases.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A sharp increase in the price of oil will most likely cause which of the following in the short run?", "choices": ["The price level falls and real output rises", "The price level rises and real output falls", "Both the price level and real output rise", "Both the price level and real output fall", "Long-run aggregate supply shifts right"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Higher input costs shift short-run aggregate supply left, raising the price level and lowering output. This combination is stagflation.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The long-run aggregate supply curve is vertical at", "choices": ["the full-employment level of real GDP", "the current level of real GDP", "a real GDP of zero", "the highest possible price level", "the level where aggregate demand is largest"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "In the long run output is determined by resources and technology, not the price level, so LRAS sits at full-employment output.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following would shift the long-run aggregate supply curve to the right?", "choices": ["An increase in consumer spending", "An increase in the price level", "A decrease in the money supply", "An increase in government transfer payments", "An improvement in technology"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Better technology raises the economy's productive capacity. The other choices affect aggregate demand or cause movement along a curve.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.1-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The graph shows an economy's aggregate demand, short-run aggregate supply, and long-run aggregate supply. Which of the following describes the economy?", "choices": ["It has an inflationary gap, and unemployment is below the natural rate.", "It is in long-run equilibrium.", "It has a recessionary gap, and unemployment is below the natural rate.", "It has a recessionary gap, and unemployment is above the natural rate.", "It has an inflationary gap, and unemployment is above the natural rate."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Current output Y1 is to the left of full-employment output YF, which is a recessionary gap with cyclical unemployment.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20460%20330%27%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%2772%27%20y2%3D%2716%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27438%27%20y2%3D%27276%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%276%27%20y%3D%2713%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EPrice%20level%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27440%27%20y%3D%27316%27%20text-anchor%3D%27end%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EReal%20GDP%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C145.9%20217.6%2C145.9%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27217.6%2C145.9%20217.6%2C276.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27104.4%2C56.0%20324.7%2C231.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.8%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27330.7%27%20y%3D%27235.0%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EAD%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27104.4%2C226.0%20337.7%2C61.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.8%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27343.7%27%20y%3D%2765.0%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESRAS%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27272.9%2C261.0%20272.9%2C36.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.8%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27278.9%27%20y%3D%2740.0%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ELRAS%3C/text%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%27217.6%27%20cy%3D%27145.9%27%20r%3D%273.6%27%20fill%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%27217.6%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27217.6%27%20y2%3D%27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27217.6%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EY1%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%27272.9%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27272.9%27%20y2%3D%27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27272.9%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EYF%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2767%27%20y1%3D%27145.9%27%20x2%3D%2772%27%20y2%3D%27145.9%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2763%27%20y%3D%27149.9%27%20text-anchor%3D%27end%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EPL1%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-3.1-07", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "If aggregate demand increases while short-run aggregate supply decreases, then in the short run", "choices": ["the price level falls and real output rises", "the price level is indeterminate and real output rises", "the price level rises and real output is indeterminate", "both the price level and real output rise", "both are indeterminate"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Both shifts push the price level up. Output rises from the demand shift but falls from the supply shift, so its change is indeterminate.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.75, the marginal propensity to save is", "choices": ["0.25", "0.75", "1.33", "4", "1.75"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "MPC + MPS = 1, so MPS = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The marginal propensity to consume is 0.8. If government spending increases by $20 billion, aggregate demand can increase by a maximum of", "choices": ["$16 billion", "$20 billion", "$25 billion", "$80 billion", "$100 billion"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "The spending multiplier is 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5. Multiplying by $20 billion gives $100 billion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The marginal propensity to consume is 0.75. A $10 billion tax cut will increase aggregate demand by a maximum of", "choices": ["$7.5 billion", "$10 billion", "$30 billion", "$40 billion", "$75 billion"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The tax multiplier is MPC/MPS = 0.75/0.25 = 3. A $10 billion tax cut raises aggregate demand by $30 billion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The marginal propensity to consume is 0.8. The government increases spending by $50 billion and raises taxes by $50 billion. Aggregate demand will change by", "choices": ["$0", "$50 billion", "$200 billion", "$250 billion", "−$50 billion"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Spending raises AD by 5 × 50 = $250 billion and the tax cuts it by 4 × 50 = $200 billion, a net $50 billion. The balanced-budget multiplier is 1.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "An economy has a recessionary gap. With no policy action, in the long run", "choices": ["nominal wages fall, short-run aggregate supply shifts right, and output returns to full employment", "nominal wages rise and short-run aggregate supply shifts left", "aggregate demand shifts right on its own", "long-run aggregate supply shifts left", "the price level rises"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Unemployed workers eventually accept lower nominal wages, which lowers costs and shifts SRAS right until output reaches full employment at a lower price level.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "An economy is producing above its full-employment output. In the long run with no policy action,", "choices": ["short-run aggregate supply shifts right, lowering the price level", "aggregate demand shifts left on its own", "long-run aggregate supply shifts right", "output stays above full employment permanently", "short-run aggregate supply shifts left, raising the price level and returning output to full employment"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Tight labor markets push nominal wages up, which shifts SRAS left until output falls back to potential at a higher price level.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.2-07", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "An economy has a recessionary gap of $200 billion and a marginal propensity to consume of 0.75. What increase in government spending would close the gap, assuming no crowding out?", "choices": ["$25 billion", "$50 billion", "$150 billion", "$200 billion", "$800 billion"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The spending multiplier is 1/0.25 = 4, so government spending needs to rise by $200/4 = $50 billion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is an example of expansionary fiscal policy?", "choices": ["Selling government bonds", "Raising the reserve requirement", "Increasing income taxes", "Increasing government spending", "Raising the discount rate"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxes. Raising spending increases aggregate demand. The bond, reserve, and discount-rate choices are monetary policy.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "To close an inflationary gap, the appropriate fiscal policy is to", "choices": ["increase government spending", "decrease taxes", "decrease government spending or increase taxes", "buy government bonds", "lower the reserve requirement"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An inflationary gap needs aggregate demand to fall, which contractionary fiscal policy achieves by cutting spending or raising taxes.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following is an automatic stabilizer during a recession?", "choices": ["Congress passes a new highway spending bill", "Unemployment insurance payments rise as more people lose their jobs", "The central bank lowers interest rates", "The government cuts income tax rates by law", "Firms raise prices"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Unemployment benefits rise automatically when joblessness increases, supporting spending without any new legislation.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A government runs a budget deficit in a year when", "choices": ["its spending exceeds its tax revenue", "its tax revenue exceeds its spending", "its national debt falls", "exports exceed imports", "the money supply falls"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A deficit is the annual shortfall of revenue relative to spending. Deficits add to the national debt.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A key disadvantage of discretionary fiscal policy is that", "choices": ["it has no effect on aggregate demand", "it always reduces the national debt", "it is controlled by the central bank", "it cannot change taxes", "delays in recognizing problems and passing legislation can make it poorly timed"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Policymakers must recognize the problem, agree on a response, and implement it. By then economic conditions may have changed.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-3.3-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "A progressive income tax acts as an automatic stabilizer because during an expansion", "choices": ["tax revenue falls automatically", "tax rates are cut by law", "transfer payments rise", "tax revenue rises more than proportionally, slowing the growth of spending", "government spending rises automatically"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "As incomes rise, more income falls into higher brackets, so taxes rise faster than income and dampen the increase in aggregate demand.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "When money is used to buy goods and services, it is serving as a", "choices": ["store of value", "unit of account", "medium of exchange", "standard of deferred payment", "form of credit"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Money's role as a medium of exchange lets people trade without needing a double coincidence of wants.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is included in M1?", "choices": ["Savings deposits", "Checkable deposits", "Government bonds", "Credit card limits", "Small time deposits"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "M1 includes currency in circulation and checkable deposits, the most liquid forms of money.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If market interest rates rise, the price of existing bonds will", "choices": ["fall", "rise", "stay the same", "rise and then fall", "equal the new interest rate"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Older bonds pay fixed coupons that now look worse than new bonds, so their prices fall until their yields match the market.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following assets is the most liquid?", "choices": ["A house", "Corporate stock", "A government bond", "A certificate of deposit", "Currency"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Currency can be spent immediately without any loss of value, which makes it the most liquid asset.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following is included in M2 but not in M1?", "choices": ["Currency", "Checkable deposits", "Traveler's checks", "Savings deposits", "Coins"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "M2 adds less liquid assets such as savings deposits, small time deposits, and money market funds to M1.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.2-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A bank has $50,000 in checkable deposits and the required reserve ratio is 20 percent. Its required reserves are", "choices": ["$2,500", "$10,000", "$20,000", "$40,000", "$50,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Required reserves equal the required reserve ratio times deposits: 0.20 × $50,000 = $10,000.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.2-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The balance sheet shows a commercial bank. If the required reserve ratio is 10 percent, what are the bank's excess reserves?", "choices": ["$0", "$5,000", "$10,000", "$15,000", "$20,000"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Required reserves are 0.10 × $100,000 = $10,000. The bank holds $15,000, so excess reserves are $5,000.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20524%20136%27%20width%3D%27524%27%20height%3D%27136%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27524%27%20height%3D%27136%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27514%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2788.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EAssets%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27208.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27336.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ELiabilities%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27468.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27514%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2788.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EReserves%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27208.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2415%2C000%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27336.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ECheckable%20deposits%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27468.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%24100%2C000%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2788.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ELoans%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27208.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2470%2C000%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27336.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EOwner%27s%20equity%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27468.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%245%2C000%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2788.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EGovernment%20bonds%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27208.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%2420%2C000%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27336.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27468.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%27124%27%20x2%3D%27514%27%20y2%3D%27124%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-4.2-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "If the required reserve ratio is 25 percent, the simple money multiplier is", "choices": ["0.25", "2.5", "4", "25", "75"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The money multiplier is 1 divided by the required reserve ratio: 1/0.25 = 4.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.2-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The required reserve ratio is 10 percent. A person deposits $1,000 of cash into a checking account at a bank that holds no excess reserves. What is the maximum amount of new loans the banking system can create?", "choices": ["$100", "$900", "$1,000", "$9,000", "$10,000"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The bank must keep $100 and can lend $900. Across the banking system, loans can grow by $900 × 10 = $9,000.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.2-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following appears as a liability on a commercial bank's balance sheet?", "choices": ["Loans to customers", "Required reserves", "Checkable deposits", "Government bonds held by the bank", "Excess reserves"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The bank owes deposits to its customers, so they are liabilities. Loans, reserves, and bonds are assets.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.3-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The demand for money slopes downward because", "choices": ["a higher price level lowers money demand", "a higher nominal interest rate raises the opportunity cost of holding money", "the central bank controls it", "money has no opportunity cost", "banks hold more reserves at higher rates"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Holding cash means giving up interest. When interest rates rise, people hold less money and more interest-earning assets.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.3-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "In a banking system with limited reserves, an increase in the price level will cause the demand for money to", "choices": ["increase and the nominal interest rate to rise", "decrease and the nominal interest rate to fall", "increase and the nominal interest rate to fall", "decrease and the nominal interest rate to rise", "stay the same"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Higher prices mean people need more money for transactions, so money demand shifts right. With a fixed supply, the interest rate rises.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.3-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "In a banking system with limited reserves, if the central bank buys government bonds, then in the money market", "choices": ["money supply decreases and the nominal interest rate rises", "money demand increases and the nominal interest rate rises", "money supply increases and the nominal interest rate falls", "money demand decreases and the nominal interest rate falls", "nothing changes"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The bond purchase adds reserves, expanding the money supply. The vertical supply curve shifts right, lowering the interest rate.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.3-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "In a banking system with ample reserves, the central bank would most likely raise its policy rate by", "choices": ["buying government bonds", "lowering the reserve requirement", "lowering the discount rate", "raising the interest rate it pays on reserve balances", "increasing government spending"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "With ample reserves, small changes in the money supply barely move rates. The central bank instead adjusts administered rates such as interest on reserves.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.3-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "An increase in real GDP, with the money supply held constant in a limited-reserves system, will most likely", "choices": ["decrease money demand and lower the nominal interest rate", "increase the money supply", "decrease the money supply", "leave the interest rate unchanged", "increase money demand and raise the nominal interest rate"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "More economic activity means more transactions, so money demand shifts right and the interest rate rises.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.4-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.4", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "In a banking system with limited reserves, which open-market operation would a central bank use to fight a recession?", "choices": ["Selling government bonds", "Buying government bonds", "Raising the discount rate", "Raising the reserve requirement", "Raising taxes"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Buying bonds adds reserves, increases the money supply, lowers interest rates, and raises aggregate demand.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.4-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.4", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following monetary policy actions would help reduce inflation in a limited-reserves system?", "choices": ["Selling government bonds", "Buying government bonds", "Lowering the discount rate", "Lowering the reserve requirement", "Cutting taxes"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Selling bonds drains reserves, shrinks the money supply, raises interest rates, and slows spending.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.4-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.4", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If the government increases its borrowing to finance a deficit, then in the loanable funds market", "choices": ["supply increases and the real interest rate falls", "demand decreases and the real interest rate falls", "demand increases and the real interest rate rises", "supply decreases and the real interest rate falls", "nothing changes"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Government borrowing adds to the demand for loanable funds, pushing the real interest rate up.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.4-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.4", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If households decide to save a larger share of their incomes, then in the loanable funds market", "choices": ["the real interest rate rises and quantity falls", "both the real interest rate and quantity rise", "both fall", "the real interest rate falls and quantity rises", "demand for loanable funds decreases"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "More saving increases the supply of loanable funds, lowering the real interest rate and raising the quantity borrowed and lent.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-4.4-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.4", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "In a banking system with ample reserves, which of the following is contractionary monetary policy?", "choices": ["Lowering the interest rate on reserve balances", "Buying government bonds", "Cutting income taxes", "Lowering the required reserve ratio", "Raising the interest rate on reserve balances"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Raising the rate paid on reserves lifts the policy rate, which raises other interest rates and reduces interest-sensitive spending.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The short-run Phillips curve shows", "choices": ["a positive relationship between inflation and unemployment", "the relationship between output and the price level", "an inverse relationship between inflation and unemployment", "the relationship between money and interest rates", "no relationship between inflation and unemployment"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "In the short run, policies that lower unemployment tend to raise inflation, and vice versa.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The long-run Phillips curve is vertical at", "choices": ["the natural rate of unemployment", "zero unemployment", "zero inflation", "the current unemployment rate", "the highest inflation rate"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "In the long run there is no trade-off: unemployment returns to its natural rate whatever the inflation rate.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "An increase in expected inflation will cause", "choices": ["the short-run Phillips curve to shift to the left", "the short-run Phillips curve to shift to the right", "the long-run Phillips curve to shift to the right", "a movement down the short-run Phillips curve", "no change"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Workers and firms build higher expected inflation into wages and prices, so every unemployment rate now comes with more inflation.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A negative supply shock such as a rise in energy prices will most likely", "choices": ["shift the short-run Phillips curve to the left", "shift the long-run Phillips curve to the left", "cause a movement along the short-run Phillips curve toward lower inflation", "shift the short-run Phillips curve to the right", "have no effect on the Phillips curve"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The shock raises both inflation and unemployment, which the Phillips curve model shows as a rightward shift of the SRPC.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "According to the quantity theory of money, if the money supply is $500 billion, velocity is 4, and the price level is 2, real output is", "choices": ["$250 billion", "$1,000 billion", "$2,000 billion", "$4,000 billion", "$125 billion"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Using MV = PY, 500 × 4 = 2 × Y, so Y = 2,000/2 = $1,000 billion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "Assuming velocity and real output are stable, a sustained increase in the money supply will, in the long run,", "choices": ["raise real output", "lower the price level", "lower the natural rate of unemployment", "raise the real interest rate permanently", "raise the price level without changing real output"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Money is neutral in the long run. With V and Y fixed, MV = PY means more money translates into proportionally higher prices.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.1-07", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The graph shows an economy at point B. Assuming the natural rate of unemployment is 5 percent and no policy is taken, which of the following will happen in the long run?", "choices": ["Expected inflation falls and unemployment stays at 3%.", "Expected inflation rises, the short-run Phillips curve shifts right, and unemployment returns to 5% at a higher inflation rate.", "The long-run Phillips curve shifts left to 3%.", "The economy moves down the short-run Phillips curve to point A.", "Inflation falls to zero."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Unemployment is below the natural rate, so inflation exceeds what people expected. As expectations rise, the SRPC shifts right until unemployment is back at 5%, with higher inflation than at A.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20460%20330%27%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%2772%27%20y2%3D%2716%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27438%27%20y2%3D%27276%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%276%27%20y%3D%2713%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EInflation%20rate%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27440%27%20y%3D%27316%27%20text-anchor%3D%27end%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EUnemployment%20rate%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C144.8%20234.0%2C144.8%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C97.9%20169.2%2C97.9%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27169.2%2C97.9%20169.2%2C276.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27104.4%2C51.0%20363.6%2C238.5%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.8%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27369.6%27%20y%3D%27242.5%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ESRPC%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27234.0%2C266.0%20234.0%2C36.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.8%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27240.0%27%20y%3D%2740.0%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3ELRPC%3C/text%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%27234.0%27%20cy%3D%27144.8%27%20r%3D%273.6%27%20fill%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27241.0%27%20y%3D%27137.8%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EA%3C/text%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%27169.2%27%20cy%3D%2797.9%27%20r%3D%273.6%27%20fill%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27176.2%27%20y%3D%2790.9%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EB%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%27169.2%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27169.2%27%20y2%3D%27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27169.2%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E3%25%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%27234.0%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27234.0%27%20y2%3D%27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27234.0%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E5%25%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "ma-5.1-08", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "If the government increases spending while the central bank sells bonds in a limited-reserves system, what happens to interest rates and real output in the short run?", "choices": ["Interest rates rise and real output is indeterminate", "Interest rates fall and real output rises", "Interest rates are indeterminate and real output rises", "Both rise", "Both fall"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Both actions push interest rates up. Output rises from the fiscal expansion but falls from the monetary contraction, so its change is indeterminate.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The difference between a budget deficit and the national debt is that", "choices": ["the deficit is the accumulated total of past debts", "they are the same thing", "the debt is measured only in real terms", "the deficit includes only interest payments", "the deficit is a single year's shortfall, while the debt is the accumulated total"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "A deficit is a flow for one year; the national debt is the stock built up from all past deficits minus surpluses.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Crowding out occurs when", "choices": ["tax cuts increase consumption", "imports replace domestic goods", "the central bank buys bonds", "government borrowing raises interest rates and reduces private investment", "private saving rises"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Deficit borrowing increases demand for loanable funds, raising the real interest rate and making some private investment unprofitable.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "Persistent crowding out is likely to slow long-run economic growth because", "choices": ["consumption falls", "the money supply shrinks", "less private investment means a smaller capital stock in the future", "exports rise", "inflation falls"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Lower investment today leaves the economy with less physical capital tomorrow, which limits future productive capacity.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Long-run economic growth can be shown by", "choices": ["a movement along the aggregate demand curve", "a rightward shift of the long-run aggregate supply curve", "a leftward shift of short-run aggregate supply", "a movement down the Phillips curve", "an increase in the price level"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Growth means a higher potential output, which is a rightward shift of LRAS and an outward shift of the production possibilities curve.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following is an investment in human capital?", "choices": ["A worker completes a technical training program", "A firm buys a new factory", "A household buys government bonds", "A family buys a new car", "A firm pays dividends"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Human capital is the knowledge and skill of workers. Training raises a worker's productivity.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "If real GDP per capita grows at 3.5 percent per year, it will double in approximately", "choices": ["3.5 years", "10 years", "20 years", "35 years", "70 years"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The rule of 70 says doubling time is about 70 divided by the growth rate: 70/3.5 = 20 years.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-07", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Which of the following government policies is most likely to promote long-run economic growth?", "choices": ["Higher tariffs on imported capital equipment", "Price controls on consumer goods", "Larger transfer payments financed by borrowing", "Restrictions on immigration of skilled workers", "Tax credits for research and development"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "R&D credits encourage new technology, which raises productivity and shifts LRAS to the right.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-08", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Productivity is best measured as", "choices": ["total output of the economy", "the number of workers employed", "total spending in the economy", "output per worker or per hour of work", "the price level divided by output"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Productivity is output per unit of input, usually per worker or per hour. It is the main driver of living standards.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-5.2-09", "subject": "macro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A country's national debt is $1,000 billion at the start of the year. During the year the government runs a budget deficit of $50 billion. At the end of the year the national debt is", "choices": ["$950 billion", "$1,000 billion", "$1,050 billion", "$1,500 billion", "$50 billion"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Each deficit adds to the stock of debt, so the debt rises to $1,000 + $50 = $1,050 billion.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.1-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is recorded in a country's current account?", "choices": ["A foreign firm buys a domestic factory", "A domestic resident buys foreign stocks", "A domestic firm exports machinery", "The central bank buys foreign currency", "A foreigner buys domestic land"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The current account records trade in goods and services, income, and transfers. Exporting machinery is trade in goods.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.1-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A foreign investor's purchase of shares in a domestic company is recorded in the domestic country's", "choices": ["current account as an export", "financial account as an inflow", "current account as an import", "financial account as an outflow", "trade balance"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Purchases of assets are financial account transactions. A foreigner buying domestic assets brings funds in.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.1-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A country with a current account deficit will generally have", "choices": ["a financial account surplus of about the same size", "a financial account deficit of about the same size", "a balanced financial account", "a government budget surplus", "a fixed exchange rate"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The balance of payments must balance. Paying for more imports than exports is financed by net sales of assets to foreigners.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.1-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Money sent home by workers employed abroad is recorded in the", "choices": ["financial account", "capital account only", "government budget", "money supply", "current account"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Remittances are transfers, which the current account records along with trade and income.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.1-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If a country's exports of services increase while everything else stays the same, its current account balance will", "choices": ["decrease", "not change", "become exactly zero", "increase", "move into deficit"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Exports of services are credits in the current account, so the balance rises.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.2-01", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "If the dollar appreciates against the euro, then", "choices": ["one dollar buys fewer euros than before", "American goods become cheaper for Europeans", "one dollar buys more euros than before", "European goods become more expensive for Americans", "US exports to Europe increase"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Appreciation means the currency gains value, so it exchanges for more of the other currency.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.2-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If American consumers buy more Japanese goods, then in the foreign exchange market for yen", "choices": ["the supply of yen increases and the yen depreciates", "the demand for yen decreases and the yen depreciates", "the demand for yen increases and the yen appreciates", "the supply of yen decreases and the yen depreciates", "nothing changes"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Americans need yen to pay for Japanese goods, so demand for yen rises and the yen gains value.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.2-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A depreciation of a country's currency will most likely", "choices": ["make its exports cheaper abroad and increase net exports", "make its exports more expensive and decrease net exports", "make its imports cheaper", "reduce aggregate demand", "have no effect on trade"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Foreigners need less of their own currency to buy the country's goods, so exports rise while imports become pricier.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.2-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The graph shows the foreign exchange market for euros. If the exchange rate is $1.10 per euro, which of the following will occur?", "choices": ["There is a surplus of euros, and the euro will depreciate.", "There is a shortage of euros, and the euro will appreciate.", "The dollar will appreciate against the euro.", "The supply of euros will decrease.", "The market is in equilibrium."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "At $1.10, below the $1.20 equilibrium, the quantity of euros demanded exceeds the quantity supplied. That shortage bids the euro's price up.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20460%20330%27%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%2772%27%20y2%3D%2716%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27438%27%20y2%3D%27276%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%276%27%20y%3D%2713%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EDollars%20per%20euro%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27440%27%20y%3D%27316%27%20text-anchor%3D%27end%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EQuantity%20of%20euros%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C88.5%20234.0%2C88.5%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C104.1%20363.6%2C104.1%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stro