her countries, which of the following will most likely occur?", "choices": ["Capital flows out and the dollar depreciates", "Capital flows in and the dollar depreciates", "Net exports rise", "The exchange rate does not change", "Capital flows in, the dollar appreciates, and net exports fall"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Higher returns attract foreign savers, who buy dollars to invest. The dollar appreciates, making US exports costlier and imports cheaper.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.3-02", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Contractionary monetary policy in a country with a flexible exchange rate will most likely cause its currency to", "choices": ["depreciate because interest rates fall", "appreciate because interest rates rise", "depreciate because interest rates rise", "appreciate because interest rates fall", "stay the same"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Tighter policy raises interest rates, drawing in foreign capital and increasing demand for the currency.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.3-03", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "An expansionary fiscal policy financed by government borrowing will affect the country's currency and net exports in which way?", "choices": ["Currency appreciates and net exports fall", "Currency depreciates and net exports rise", "Currency appreciates and net exports rise", "Currency depreciates and net exports fall", "No effect on either"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Borrowing raises real interest rates, attracting capital inflows and appreciating the currency. The stronger currency reduces net exports.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.3-04", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A depreciation of a country's currency will most likely affect aggregate demand by", "choices": ["decreasing it because imports rise", "decreasing it because exports fall", "leaving it unchanged", "increasing it because government spending rises", "increasing it because net exports rise"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Cheaper exports and pricier imports raise net exports, a component of aggregate demand.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.3-05", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If interest rates in Europe fall while US interest rates stay the same, then", "choices": ["financial capital flows from the United States to Europe and the dollar depreciates", "the euro appreciates", "financial capital flows from Europe to the United States and the dollar appreciates", "US net exports increase", "nothing happens"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "US assets now offer relatively higher returns, so investors move funds into dollars and the dollar strengthens.", "image": ""}, {"id": "ma-6.3-06", "subject": "macro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "If a country imposes a tariff that reduces its imports, then in the foreign exchange market its currency will most likely", "choices": ["depreciate because supply of the currency increases", "depreciate because demand for the currency decreases", "appreciate because demand for the currency decreases", "appreciate because supply of the currency decreases", "not change"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Residents buy fewer foreign goods, so they supply less of their own currency to obtain foreign currency. Lower supply raises its value.", "image": ""}]}
